---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Tune from "../components/Tune.astro";

// Charity not chosen yet. Set these once the family decides.
const CHARITY = { name: "", url: "" };
// The photo-essay version of this page (full-bleed photos, scroll scenes) is kept in src/drafts/photo-essay.astro.
---
<Base title="Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, 1929 to 2026">
  <Nav current="obituary" />
  <Tune />

  <header class="cover plain">
    <div class="cover-title col">
      <span class="lady" data-reveal>Lady</span>
      <h1 data-split>Margaret</h1>
      <span class="fullname" data-reveal>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · 1929 – 2026</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="col prose">
      <p class="first">Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, who left the slums of Glasgow at nineteen and remade herself in Montreal as a model, a mother of four, and, in the eyes of her family, a lady, died peacefully there on November 5, 2026, surrounded by family. She was 97.</p>
      <p>Some people pass through life quietly. Margaret was not one of them.</p>
      <p class="dropcap">Born in Glasgow in 1929, Margaret was one of seven children raised in the Garngad, one of the poorest districts in the city. Her father, Hugh Cairns, drank and was in and out of prison. Her mother, Helen Kennedy, died suddenly when Margaret was six. Then came the war, and German bombs over Glasgow. The Kennedy-Cairns children were left largely to raise themselves, and they did whatever it took to keep fed.</p>
      <p>The Garngad made her. It taught her to survive, to make do, and to rely on no one but herself. And it left her with a conviction she never lost: that the people who depended on her would never go without the way she had.</p>
      <p>She came, too, from a line of headstrong women. Her maternal grandmother, Helen Orr Kennedy, was a Worthy Mistress, a senior leader in Scotland’s women’s Orange Order, which forbade marriage to Catholics. When her daughter Helen married Hugh Cairns, an Irish Catholic immigrant, against the wishes of both families, it was a scandal, and the two sides kept their distance from then on. Margaret would not. After her mother died, she ran away again and again, across that divide, to her grandmother’s house, a modest, respectable home that after the tenements of the Garngad must have seemed palatial. She was already going wherever she believed a better life lay, whatever lines stood in her way.</p>
      <p>By her late teens she was studying to become a nun. For a motherless girl from the Garngad, the convent offered what little else did: an education, security, and a respectable way out. But it could not hold her. At nineteen she left it and crossed the Atlantic to begin a new life in Montreal with her fiancé, Henry, Canadian-born but also of Irish and Scottish parents, whom she had known only briefly.</p>
      <p>It left an ocean between her and the brothers and sisters with whom she had survived her childhood.</p>
      <p>Through her twenties she raised three children in that first marriage, in a new country, with no one of her own to turn to. Her husband had suffered a brain injury and was, by every account, a difficult man. She had grown up in the shadow of one troubled man and now found herself married to another. In the late 1950s she divorced him. For a woman of that era, raised Catholic, with three small children and no family within three thousand miles, it was an enormous risk. She took it.</p>
      <p>What followed were the hardest years of her adult life. Not yet thirty, divorced, and alone with three young children, Margaret worked as a clerk by day and cleaned offices by night. She was determined that her children would never know the poverty she had, whatever it cost her. So, though it stretched her to the limit, she rented in Westmount, one of the city’s most exclusive neighbourhoods, for the sake of its schools, and she kept up appearances so well that no one there ever guessed how close to the bone the family was living. Along the way she modelled, and it was easy to see why: she had striking Scottish looks, vivid red hair, and a smile that could light up a room.</p>
      <p>At the turn of the 1960s she married Ted Liontos, a Greek photographer with dashing, James Bond good looks, and in May 1961 their daughter, Anthea, was born. The marriage lasted only a few years. Before long Margaret was raising her children alone again, four of them now.</p>
      <p>Through the 1960s and 1970s she worked behind the counters of Montreal’s finest stores, among them Birks, the storied jeweller, where her magnetism made her a natural. Suitors were never in short supply, but any man who wanted Margaret without her children was shown the door. They came together or not at all. With Ted’s help, Anthea went to private school, and in time became the first in the family to go to university, at McGill. A generation after the Garngad, her daughter was at McGill.</p>
      <p>Then, rare for a single mother of the time, she bought a house of her own in Westmount. The woman who had once rented there on a clerk’s pay and a cleaner’s became a familiar face in the city’s anglophone society, holding her own among its best-connected families with an ease no one would have traced to a Glasgow tenement.</p>
      <p>She was glamorous, opinionated, endlessly sociable, and could fill a room before she had properly entered it. She loved beautiful things, good conversation, and making an impression. She was quick to laugh, occasionally quicker to anger, and her Scottish wit was as sharp as her opinions. And she was rarely far from a song. She had a beautiful voice, part of a musical family that also gave the world her niece, the Scottish singer Lulu, and it filled the family home.</p>
      <p>In the 1970s, amid the Westmount circles where it had a loyal following, Margaret found Christian Science. Born into a religious divide she had never chosen, she now chose a faith for herself, and embraced it wholly. Mary Baker Eddy taught that spiritual reality, not material circumstance, is the truth of a person, and that lack is an illusion. Margaret had spent her life refusing to be defined by her circumstances; here was a faith that told her she had been right all along. She was rarely shy about it, and a conversation with Margaret, whether with a grandson, a neighbour, or a nurse she had known for ten minutes, could soon turn to the healing power of right thinking.</p>
      <p>In the early 1980s she lost her son Colin and her daughter Helen, both in their twenties, within thirteen months of each other. Everything she had built, she had built to protect her children. This was the one thing she could not protect them from. She carried that grief for the rest of her life, and still she did not let it close her off from beauty, laughter, or the company of others.</p>
      <p>Years later, when Anthea was raising a young son on her own while working in Manhattan, Margaret knew that life better than anyone, and she would not hear of leaving him to strangers. Decades after crossing an ocean to build a future for herself, she left her beloved Montreal for New York City to help build one for her grandson, Sean, and helped raise him herself.</p>
      <p>The years did nothing to dull her. Well into her eighties she loved a practical joke, never more than at Christmas, when a little Tiffany box in a stocking might turn out to hold a set of false teeth or a few toenail clippings. And she never lost her gift for turning an ordinary encounter into an occasion: left on her own for a few hours on the Amalfi Coast, she came back the best of friends with a countess she had only just met.</p>
      <p>In later life, her son Paul gifted her a small plot of land in her native Scotland, along with a certificate naming her Lady Margaret. She was touched by the thought but never entirely comfortable with the title, which struck her as faintly fraudulent. The irony was lost on no one else. She had become more of a lady than most people will ever meet, and she had needed no certificate to do it.</p>
      <p>In her final years, age took from her the self-sufficiency she had prized since the Garngad. For a woman who had always been the one others depended upon, learning to depend on them was no small thing. Her family stayed close, grateful for the chance to give back some of what she had given them. And she was still singing in her very last days.</p>
      <p>She left Glasgow with little more than her wits. She leaves behind a family that was given every chance she never had, because she would not have it any other way.</p>
      <p>She was predeceased by her children Colin and Helen, and by her six brothers and sisters, Nellie, Betty, James, Davey, Hugh Jr., and Colin. She is survived by her son, Paul; her daughter, Anthea; and her grandson, Sean, who will carry her memory forward with love, laughter, and gratitude.</p>
      <p>In accordance with her wishes, Margaret will be cremated, and at a private family ceremony her ashes will be scattered where she will join Colin and Helen once more.</p>
      <p>It will come as no surprise that, even in her final days, her wit was as sharp as ever. When nurses explained that they needed to take medical images of her ribs, the former model asked, without missing a beat, “Am I being paid for these pictures?”</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col prose to-book">
      <p><a href="/memories">Share a memory of Margaret, or read what others have written, in the memory book.</a></p>
    </div>

    {CHARITY.name && (
      <section class="col prose tribute">
        <p>In lieu of flowers, the family asks that you consider a donation to {CHARITY.name}, a cause close to Margaret’s heart.</p>
        <a class="btn" href={CHARITY.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donate to {CHARITY.name}</a>
      </section>
    )}
  </main>

  <footer class="col">
    <p>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · Glasgow 1929 · Montreal 2026</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/motion.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
